  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This eLife article has an early but credible scholarly footprint. eLife assessed it as \u201cImportant\u201d and \u201cConvincing,\u201d and the record already shows two citing works, one supporting scite citation, and three documented downstream reuses, suggesting that the study is being taken up in related research on publication inequality and gendered career outcomes.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Chaoqun Ni, Isabel Basson, Giovanna Badia, Nathalie Tufenkji, Cassidy R. Sugimoto, and Vincent Larivi\u00e8re examine a key mechanism behind publication disparities in elite journals: differences in submission behavior. Based on a survey of nearly 5,000 active authors, the article argues that women are less likely than men to submit to journals of the highest impact factor, while reported desk rejection does not appear to differ by gender in the study\u2019s assessment. That framing shifts attention from peer-review outcomes alone to the earlier decision to submit, making the paper relevant to debates about how gender inequality is produced across the publication pipeline.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest impact signal in the record is the peer-reviewed assessment from eLife, which describes the study as important and convincing. The article also has an early citation trail: OpenAlex records two citations, one in 2024 and one in 2025. Those citing works come from education and biomedical-research contexts, which suggests the paper is already informing discussion beyond metascience alone, including work on publication prestige and diversity in research fields.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Reuse indicators reinforce that pattern. The article has been reused in three documented ways, including one article, one preprint, and one biomedical article mention in Europe PMC. scite also records one supporting citation and four mentioning citations, which indicates that the paper is being discussed and built upon, even though the overall citation count remains modest at this stage.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The surrounding record points to careful stewardship and accessibility. The article is gold open access, and the associated research data are linked through a Zenodo deposit and a GitHub repository. Public-discourse and event-data sources are currently unavailable in the record, so the story here is primarily one of early scholarly uptake rather than broader public visibility.</p>",
